el alignment (CKA) with a linear kernel instead uses exactly\nthe phoneme-balanced held-out frame indices used by KNN:\nvalid MFA-aligned frames from the same 100 utterances, 40\nphoneme classes, and at most 500 frames per class. All configurations share utterances, frame indices, and random seed.\nFor S = [s1 , . . . , sN ]⊤ and Z = [z1 , . . . , zN ]⊤ , we center each\nfeature column and compute\n\nForced alignment. We resample LibriSpeech audio to 16\nkHz and obtain phoneme boundaries using Montreal Forced\nAligner (MFA) v3.3.9 with the English (US) ARPA acoustic\nmodel v3.0.0 and English (US) ARPA pronunciation dictionary\nv3.0.0. Utterances without a corresponding MFA-generated\nTextGrid are excluded from the phoneme analysis, and empty\nphone intervals are ignored. Stress markers are removed before\nARPA vowels are merged into the final phoneme classes. The\nresulting boundaries are converted to frame-level labels at each\nencoder’s native frame rate. The analysis covers 40 phoneme\nclasses; to reduce class imbalance, at most 500 frames per\nphoneme are sampled from each split with the same fixed seed.\n\nCKA(S, Z) =\n\n∥S ⊤ Z∥2F\n.\n⊤\n∥S S∥F ∥Z ⊤ Z∥F\n\n(A3)\n\nCKA uses no variance standardization, PCA, or ℓ2 normalization. KNN retains the standardization, PCA, and reference/test procedure described above; KNN retention is\nKNN(z)/KNN(s) with KNN(s) = 0.6468.\n\nToken–Phoneme Co-occurrence\nRepresentation Extraction and Standardization\n\nToken–phoneme co-occurrence uses the same MFA-aligned,\nEvery encoder is frozen and evaluated without gradient updates. phoneme-balanced held-out frames as the representation visuFor codecs and tokenizers, we extract the continuous repre- alizations and KNN analysis. Each representation is discretized\n8\n\n\fby its separately trained probing vector quantizer (P-VQ) under\nthe shared 8K (8,192-codeword) configuration in Appendix B.\nFor phoneme p and token k, we count\nX\nCp,k =\n⊮[yi = p, ki = k],\n(A4)\n\nSetting\n\nValue\n\nCodebook size\n8,192\nCodeword dimension\n8\nInput/output projections\nd→8/8→d\nTraining steps\n200K\nSegment duration\n3 seconds\nBatch size\n16\nReconstruction objective\nℓ2 feature reconstruction\nCommitment weight\n1.0\nk-means initialization\n50 iterations\nEMA update\nEnabled; identical across probes\nInactive-code replacement Enabled; identical across probes\nReplacement source\nShared feature buffer\nOptimizer and schedule\nIdentical across probes\nWaveform decoder\nNone\n\ni\n\nP\nand normalize each token column as C̄p,k = Cp,k / p′ Cp′ ,k .\nToken columns are grouped by the phoneme with maximum\nnormalized co-occurrence. A concentrated column therefore\nindicates that the corresponding token is consistently associated\nwith one phoneme, whereas diffuse off-block mass indicates\nsharing across phonemes. Appendix Figures A3–A4 show all\neight representations.\n\nAppendix B: Probing Quantizer and Token Language-Model Details\n\nTable A1: Shared probing-quantizer configuration.\nSetting\n\nProbing Quantizer\n\nValue\n\nBackbone\nQwen2-1.5B\nMaximum sequence length\n2,048\nLearning rate\n10−4\nWarmup steps\n500\nTraining epochs\n3\nGlobal batch size\n64\nTraining split\nLibriSpeech train-960\nEvaluation splits\nLibriSpeech test-clean/test-other\nReported metrics\nP-ACC and P-PPL\n\nNative quantizers vary in codebook size, codebook count, quantizer family, and training objective, so native-token predictability\nconflates representation quality with quantizer capacity and\ndesign. We therefore bypass native quantizers where present\nand attach a matched probing quantizer to every representation. For a standardized representation h̃ ∈ Rd , the trainable\ninput projection maps d → 8, the single-codebook P-VQ uses\nexponential moving average (EMA) updates to assign one of\nTable A2: Shared token language-model configuration.\n8,192 codewords in R8 , and the trainable output projection\nmaps 8 → d. Thus, representation dimensionality does not\nchange the discrete capacity of the probe. The output projection\nreconstructs the continuous representation only; P-VQ contains shared Qwen2-1.5B recipe. P-PPL measures distribution-level\nmodeling difficulty, while P-ACC provides an intuitive exactno waveform decoder.\nprediction measure. Appendix Table A5 instead reports nativeThe probing objective is\ntoken accuracy (N-ACC) for the two ReLMCodec reconstruction\n2\nLprobe = Pout (q) − h̃ + λcommit Lcommit ,\n(A5) quantizers compared there; these metrics characterize differ2\nently optimized token spaces and are not directly comparawith λcommit = 1. Encoder features are detached, and only the ble. Table 2 in the main text uses released checkpoints for\nprojections and probing quantizer are optimized. All represen- baseline codecs and our trained checkpoints for ReLMCodec,\ntations use the same data processing, number of updates, and while non-waveform representations in Appendix Figure A1\nuse the matched-decoder diagnostic described in Appendix C.\nquantizer configuration.\nInactive entries are replaced from a feature buffer populated by Codebook perplexity (CB-PPL) is exp(H(p(k))) under the\nrecent training batches. k-means initialization, EMA codebook assignments used by the corresponding row and is unrelated to\nupdates, and inactive-code replacement are identical across all P-PPL.\nprobes and prevent configuration differences from confounding\nthe representation comparison.\n\nAutoregressive Token Language Model\n\nAppendix C: Baselines and Reconstruction Protocols\n\nEach separately trained P-VQ produces a single token sequence\nat the representation’s native frame rate. The same Qwen2-1.5B\nautoregressive model [Yang et al., 2024a] and training recipe\nare used for every representation. The model uses LibriSpeech\ntrain-960 and the test splits; the 500/100 phoneme-analysis\nsamples do not define or alter these language-model splits.\nProbe-ACC (P-ACC) and Probe-PPL (P-PPL) are top-1 nexttoken accuracy and perplexity from P-VQ tokens under the\n\nEnd-to-end codecs. All baseline codecs in Table 2 of the main\ntext are evaluated using released complete checkpoints, including their original encoders, quantizers, and waveform decoders;\nno baseline component is retrained or replaced. ReLMCodec\nrows use our trained checkpoints. Reconstructions on test-clean\nand test-other are resampled to 16 kHz and scored with the\nsame metric implementations.\nThe baseline checkpoints correspond to DAC [Kumar et al.,\n9\n\n\f2023], SpeechTokenizer [Zhang et al., 2024], X-Codec [Ye et al.,\n2025a], Stable Codec [Parker et al., 2025], XY-Tokenizer [Gong\net al., 2026], mimi [Défossez et al., 2024], Qwen3-TTSTokenizer [Hu et al., 2026a], UniCodec [Jiang et al., 2025],\nWavTokenizer [Ji et al., 2025], X-Codec2 [Ye et al., 2025b],\nAUV [Chen et al., 2026], SemantiCodec [Liu et al., 2024], and\nFocalCodec [Della Libera et al., 2025].\nThe downstream text-to-speech (TTS) comparison additionally includes BigCodec [Xin et al., 2024].\nMatched-decoder diagnostics. The representation analysis\nalso includes systems that are not complete waveform codecs.\nWavLM [Chen et al., 2022], HuBERT [Hsu et al., 2021], and\nW2v-BERT [Seamless Communication et al., 2023] release\nencoders only. MaskGCT [Wang et al., 2025] includes an\nencoder, quantizer, and semantic decoder, but that decoder\nreconstructs continuous SSL features rather than waveforms.\nFor reconstruction-based diagnostics such as SIM, we freeze\nthe released representation modules and train the same waveform decoder with identical data, optimization, updates, and\nevaluation settings. These results measure representation reconstruction compatibility and appear only as diagnostic references\nin Appendix Figure A1; they are not Table 2 codec baselines.\nRepresentation-level predictability separately uses P-VQ, except for Appendix Table A5, which directly evaluates the two\nReLMCodec quantizers.\n\nUniCodec\n\n2.4\n\nPESQ\n\nBest non-ReLMCodec codec ACC\nMaskGCT: 5.88%\n\nReLMCodec@64k\n\nAUV\nWavTokenizer\n\n2.2\n\n50 TPS\n75 TPS\n80 TPS\n\nMaskGCT\n\nBest SSL reconstruction\n\nEvaluation Metric Definitions\nPredictability and phoneme structure. P-ACC and P-PPL\nmeasure top-1 next-token accuracy and perplexity under the\nmatched P-VQ protocol; higher P-ACC and lower P-PPL indicate easier autoregressive modeling. KNN accuracy, Silhouette, Davies–Bouldin, and V-measure quantify pre-quantization\nphoneme organization as detailed in Appendices A–B.\nReconstruction and downstream TTS. WER is computed\nwith Whisper-Large-v3 [Radford et al., 2022]. Multi-scale\nlog-mel spectrogram loss (Log-Mel) compares reference and reconstructed speech at multiple time–frequency resolutions. SIM\nis the similarity between WavLM speaker embeddings [Chen\net al., 2022]; PESQ and STOI estimate perceptual quality and\nintelligibility, and UTMOS predicts speech naturalness [Saeki\net al., 2022]. Lower WER and Log-Mel are better; higher SIM,\nPESQ, STOI, and UTMOS are better. The downstream TTS\nevaluation reports WER, SIM, and UTMOS under the shared\ngeneration setup.\nHuman evaluation. H-MOS is the human mean opinion score\nfor reconstructed speech; higher ratings indicate better perceived\nquality. Appendix Figure A5 reports rating distributions together\nwith their medians and means.\n\nReLMCodec@8k\n\nX-Codec2\n\n2.0\n1.8\n\nBigCodec\n\nas follows: NeuCodec is the finite scalar quantization (FSQ)\ncodec introduced by Julian et al. [2025]; S3Tokenizer is the\nsupervised semantic tokenizer introduced by CosyVoice [Du\net al., 2024a]; and MingTok-Audio is the continuous tokenizer\nintroduced by Ming-UniAudio [Yan et al., 2025]. DiscreteWavLM6-KM uses the official discrete_wavlm_large release with layer_ids=[6] associated with SELM [Wang\net al., 2024], while SpeechTokenizer-Snake uses the official speechtokenizer_snake checkpoint from SpeechTokenizer [Zhang et al., 2024].\n\nw2v-BERT-L17\nHuBERT-L11\n(ACC=21.51%)\n\n1.6\nFocalCodec\n\n1.4\n2\n\n4\n\nWavLM-L24\n(ACC=34.27%)", "stderr": "", "interrupted": false, "isImage": false, "noOutputExpected": false}}
