ette versus P-PPL (p = 0.002).\n\nResidual-Scale Intervention\nMain-paper Figure 4 visualizes P-ACC and PESQ across\nthe five residual scales. At α = 5, KNN/P-ACC/WER are\n0.5847/6.76/4.11, and SIM/PESQ/UTMOS are 0.753/2.19/4.12.\nAt α = 1, they are 0.6035/7.04/4.18 and 0.749/2.18/4.09,\nrespectively. At α = 0.5, they are 0.6299/9.31/4.05 and\n0.751/2.17/4.05. At α = 0.1, they are 0.6439/9.63/4.16 and\n0.749/2.17/4.03. At α = 0.01, they are 0.6465/11.43/5.78 and\n0.699/2.05/3.85. Every P-ACC uses a separately trained P-VQ\nrather than native codec token IDs. Across α > 0.1, PESQ\nchanges by at most 0.02, while P-ACC falls to 6.76 at α = 5;\ndecreasing α to 0.01 improves P-ACC but degrades WER,\nSIM, PESQ, and UTMOS. We therefore select α = 0.1 as the\nbalanced operating point.\n\nTeacher-Selection Analysis\nWith the W2v-BERT 2.0 L17 main path fixed, replacing the\nW2v-BERT teacher with WavLM-Large L24 improves P-ACC\nand WER, leaves SIM nearly unchanged, and decreases PESQ\nslightly. WavLM-Large L24 as the main path raises KNN and\nP-ACC but worsens WER, SIM, and PESQ. This two-model\ncomparison supports the selected asymmetric assignment but\n\nAppendix E: Computational Cost and\nInference Efficiency\nBenchmark Protocol\nAll models are evaluated in 32-bit floating point (FP32) with\nbatch size 1 on 10-second, 16-kHz waveform inputs. We use\none NVIDIA GeForce RTX 4090D GPU with 23.52 GiB memory and 15 allocated Intel Xeon Platinum 8474C CPU cores,\nrunning Ubuntu 22.04.5 LTS. The software environment uses\nPython 3.12.3, PyTorch 2.8.0 with CUDA 12.8, and cuDNN 9.1.\nEach measurement follows 10 warm-up iterations and 30 timed\nruns. Table A6 reports median real-time factor (RTF), with\n10th–90th percentile (P10–P90) intervals in brackets, together\nwith parameter counts and profiler-estimated floating-point operations (FLOPs). The accompanying benchmark metadata also\nrecords means, standard deviations, quartiles, and interquartile\nranges for every stage.\nThe input waveform tensor is resident on the GPU before\ntiming. RTF includes SSL feature extraction, model-internal\npreprocessing and transfers, PAPA, quantization, and waveform\ndecoding where applicable. It excludes audio file I/O, waveform loading and resampling, the initial host-to-device transfer,\nand model loading. We call torch.cuda.synchronize()\nimmediately before and after each timed invocation. Encode,\ndecode, and end-to-end latency are measured independently;\ntheir medians are therefore not expected to be additive.\nRTF interquartile ranges (×103 ; encode/decode/endto-end) are 0.0069/0.0020/0.1693 for ReLMCodec@8K,\n0.1577/0.0031/0.2874\nfor\nReLMCodec@64K,\nand\n0.0088/0.0037/0.1009 for X-Codec2. ReLMCodec@8K\nmatches X-Codec2 in median end-to-end RTF (10.057 versus\n\n15\n\n\fModel\nReLMCodec@8K\nReLMCodec@64K\nX-Codec2\nModel\n\nYushen Chen, Kai Hu, Long Zhou, Shulin Feng, Xusheng\nYang, Hangting Chen, and Xie Chen. AUV: Teaching audio\nuniversal vector quantization with single nested codebook.\nIn 2026 IEEE International Conference on Acoustics, Speech\nand Signal Processing (ICASSP), pages 17027–17031. IEEE,\n2026.\n\nParams (M) FLOPs / 1 s (G)\n\nStage\n\n782.74\n782.74\n822.74\n\n61.74\n61.78\n65.39\nMedian [P10, P90]\n\nReLMCodec@8K\n\nEncode\n9.994 [9.986, 10.001]\nDecode\n0.550 [0.548, 0.552]\nEnd-to-end 10.057 [9.987, 10.267]\nReLMCodec@64K Encode\n9.987 [9.710, 10.005]\nDecode\n0.555 [0.552, 0.557]\nEnd-to-end 10.324 [10.205, 10.967]\nX-Codec2\nEncode\n9.993 [9.565, 9.999]\nDecode\n0.935 [0.933, 0.942]\nEnd-to-end 10.070 [9.968, 10.148]\n\nChanghao Cheng, Wei Wang, Wangyou Zhang, Dongya Jia,\nJian Wu, Zhuo Chen, and Yanmin Qian. On the distillation\nloss functions of speech VAE for unified reconstruction,\nunderstanding, and generation, 2026. URL https://arxiv.org/\nabs/2604.12383.\n\nAlexandre Défossez, Jade Copet, Gabriel Synnaeve, and Yossi\nAdi. High fidelity neural audio compression, 2022. URL\nTable A6: Inference efficiency comparison. RTF